# Tauri Architecture Patterns and Development Guidelines

## Overview
Juno follows established Tauri v2 patterns with multi-agent AI orchestration, voice control, and advanced tool management. The architecture emphasizes security, performance, and maintainability through consistent patterns.

## Project Structure

### Core Architecture
```
src-tauri/
├── src/
│   ├── lib.rs                    # Main Tauri app setup and global shortcuts
│   ├── state.rs                  # Central AppState container
│   ├── anthropic.rs              # AI orchestrator and main agent entry point
│   ├── commands/                 # Tauri command handlers
│   │   ├── core.rs              # Core app commands (settings, etc.)
│   │   ├── tools.rs             # Tool configuration commands
│   │   ├── orchestrator.rs      # Task orchestration commands
│   │   ├── memory.rs            # Memory management commands
│   │   └── dev/                 # Development-specific commands
│   ├── agent/                   # AI agent implementations
│   │   ├── tools/               # Tool providers and implementations
│   │   ├── implementations/     # Specialized agent types
│   │   └── prompts/             # Agent prompt templates
│   ├── voice_control/           # Voice input monitoring
│   │   ├── dictation_monitor.rs # Spacebar hold for dictation
│   │   └── agent_monitor.rs     # Option+D hold for agent
│   └── cloud/                   # External service integrations
└── tauri.conf.json              # Tauri configuration
```

### Frontend Integration
```
src/
├── App.tsx                      # Main React app with event listeners
├── hooks/
│   └── useSettings.ts           # Settings state management
├── components/
│   ├── settings/                # Settings UI components
│   └── ui/                      # Reusable UI components
└── types/                       # TypeScript type definitions
```

## Command Pattern Implementation

### Tauri Commands Structure
All commands follow this pattern in [src-tauri/src/commands/](mdc:src-tauri/src/commands):
```rust
use tauri::{AppHandle, command};
use crate::state::AppState;

#[command]
pub async fn command_name(
    app: AppHandle,
    param: ParamType,
) -> Result<ReturnType, String> {
    // 1. Access state
    let app_state = app.state::<AppState>();

    // 2. Perform operation
    let result = perform_operation(&app_state, param)?;

    // 3. Update state if needed
    app_state.update_something(result.clone());

    // 4. Emit events if needed
    app.emit_all("event-name", &result)?;

    // 5. Return result
    Ok(result)
}
```

### Command Registration
Commands registered in [src-tauri/src/lib.rs](mdc:src-tauri/src/lib.rs):
```rust
fn main() {
    tauri::Builder::default()
        .manage(AppState::default())
        .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![
            // Core commands
            commands::core::get_agent_trigger_mode,
            commands::core::set_agent_trigger_mode,

            // Tool commands
            commands::tools::get_tool_config,
            commands::tools::set_tool_config,

            // Memory commands
            commands::memory::get_memory_status,

            // Other command modules...
        ])
        .setup(|app| {
            setup_global_shortcuts(&app.handle())?;
            initialize_monitors(&app.handle())?;
            Ok(())
        })
        .run(tauri::generate_context!())
        .expect("error while running tauri application");
}
```

## State Management Architecture

### AppState Pattern
Central state container in [src-tauri/src/state.rs](mdc:src-tauri/src/state.rs):
```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

#[derive(Default)]
pub struct AppState {
    // Core monitors
    dictation_monitor: Arc<Mutex<DictationMonitor>>,
    agent_monitor: Arc<Mutex<AgentMonitor>>,

    // Settings
    agent_trigger_mode: Arc<Mutex<AgentTriggerMode>>,

    // Managers
    memory_manager: Arc<Mutex<AdvancedMemoryManager>>,
    tool_config_manager: Arc<Mutex<ToolConfigManager>>,

    // Runtime state
    is_agent_active: Arc<Mutex<bool>>,
    current_session_id: Arc<Mutex<Option<String>>>,
}

impl AppState {
    // Getter pattern - always clone for thread safety
    pub fn get_agent_trigger_mode(&self) -> AgentTriggerMode {
        self.agent_trigger_mode.lock().unwrap().clone()
    }

    // Setter pattern - update state and emit events
    pub fn set_agent_trigger_mode(&self, mode: AgentTriggerMode) {
        *self.agent_trigger_mode.lock().unwrap() = mode;
    }

    // Manager access pattern
    pub fn with_memory_manager<R, F>(&self, f: F) -> R
    where
        F: FnOnce(&mut AdvancedMemoryManager) -> R,
    {
        let mut manager = self.memory_manager.lock().unwrap();
        f(&mut *manager)
    }
}
```

### Thread Safety Guidelines
- **Always use Arc<Mutex<T>>** for shared state
- **Clone values when returning** from getters
- **Keep lock scope minimal** to prevent deadlocks
- **Use with_* methods** for complex manager operations

## Error Handling Patterns

### AgentError Enum
Centralized error handling in [src-tauri/src/agent/mod.rs](mdc:src-tauri/src/agent/mod.rs):
```rust
#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum AgentError {
    #[error("Anthropic API error: {0}")]
    AnthropicError(String),

    #[error("Tool execution failed: {0}")]
    ToolError(String),

    #[error("Memory management error: {0}")]
    MemoryError(String),

    #[error("Voice control error: {0}")]
    VoiceError(String),

    #[error("State error: {0}")]
    StateError(String),
}
```

### Error Handling in Commands
```rust
#[command]
pub async fn risky_operation(app: AppHandle) -> Result<String, String> {
    // Convert AgentError to String for Tauri
    perform_operation()
        .await
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())
}

// Internal error handling
async fn perform_operation() -> Result<String, AgentError> {
    let result = dangerous_operation()
        .map_err(|e| AgentError::ToolError(format!("Operation failed: {}", e)))?;

    Ok(result)
}
```

